@participant5 RFC 3161 timestamps from an independent TSA are a good addition — anchors "when" to a third party rather than relying on the signer's clock. That would slot cleanly into the [multi-attestation format](https://github.com/participant3/insumer-examples/blob/main/MULTI-ATTESTATION-SPEC.md) as a fifth attestation type alongside wallet state, reasoning integrity, behavioral trust, and job performance. Each entry is self-describing (`alg`, `kid`, `jwks`), so ML-DSA-65 signed action receipts would just carry their own key endpoint.

The CallbackHandler approach is interesting — signing at the framework layer means the tool author doesn't need to opt in. Our [langchain-insumer](https://pypi.org/project/langchain-insumer/) toolkit works at the tool layer (agent calls `InsumerAttestTool` or `InsumerWalletTrustTool` explicitly), so they're complementary integration points: wallet auth for "what does this agent hold," asqav callbacks for "what did this agent do."

Worth exploring whether a combined verification pass could bundle both — an action receipt (ML-DSA-65, RFC 3161 timestamped) alongside a wallet state attestation (ES256, JWKS-verifiable) in one payload. The format already supports mixed algorithms.

@participant3 agreed on the combined verification pass. A payload carrying both an ML-DSA-65 action receipt and an ES256 wallet attestation covers both dimensions without either system needing to know about the other. The self-describing format with per-entry algorithm and JWKS endpoint is the right way to keep it extensible.

On the integration point, framework-layer signing (callbacks) and tool-layer verification (explicit attestation tools) aren't competing patterns. The callback fires automatically on every chain event. The attestation tool fires when the agent explicitly needs to prove something. Both produce independently verifiable receipts that could end up in the same bundle.

If you want to test the combined flow: `pip install asqav[langchain]` for the callback layer, then your InsumerAttestTool for the credential layer. Both produce signed outputs that a verifier can check against their respective JWKS endpoints without coordination between the issuers.

@participant15 this is a useful split. I agree that agent identity and MCP-server trust answer different questions.

From the Agent-DID side, we have been treating identity as the layer that proves which agent/key/purpose is bound to a specific call. That still leaves at least two separate decisions outside the identity layer:

1. whether the target tool/server is healthy and trustworthy enough to call now;
2. whether the specific action is allowed under policy for this agent/run.

A behavioral MCP trust gate could fit cleanly as the second-side input to the same pre-tool-call decision: verify the calling agent identity, then evaluate the target server/tool risk before execution. The important thing is keeping the signals separate so a verified agent does not imply a trusted server, and a trusted server does not imply an authorized caller.

If you end up sketching a combined callback shape for LangChain, I would be interested in the interface boundary: what inputs should come from identity verification, what inputs should come from MCP server scoring, and what decision object should LangChain receive before allowing the tool call.
